.TH ECLIPSE 1 "2026" "Eclipse 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
eclipse \- a friendly interactive Unix shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eclipse
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ SCRIPT
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ...]]
.br
.B eclipse \-c
.I COMMAND
.RI [ NAME
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B eclipse
is an interactive command interpreter. It runs ordinary Linux programs
with pipes, redirection, job control, variables and globbing, and adds
plain-language commands, inline suggestions from history, Tab
completion and helpful error messages.
.PP
Started with no
.I SCRIPT
and a terminal on standard input, Eclipse is interactive. Otherwise it
runs the commands in
.IR SCRIPT ,
or those read from standard input, and exits.
.PP
Eclipse is not a scripting language: it has no
.BR if ,
.BR for ,
.B while
or functions. Scripts written for
.BR sh (1)
or
.BR bash (1)
keep working, because the system runs them with the interpreter named
on their first line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " COMMAND"
Run
.I COMMAND
and exit. Further arguments become
.BR $0 ,
.BR $1 ,
and so on.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
Do not read the configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read
.I FILE
instead of the default configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of options.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH EVERYDAY COMMANDS
.TP
.BR go " [\fIFOLDER\fR]"
Change directory. With no argument, go home. If
.I FOLDER
is not found here, Eclipse also tries a match that ignores case and a
folder of that name in the home directory, and prints where it went.
Several words are joined, so
.B go My Folder
works without quotes.
.TP
.BR up " [\fILEVELS\fR]"
Move up one directory, or
.I LEVELS
directories.
.TP
.B back
Return to the directory you were in before. Repeating it walks further
back along the trail.
.TP
.B whereami
Print the current directory.
.TP
.BI mkcd " FOLDER"
Create a directory, with any missing parents, and enter it.
.TP
.BI findfile " NAME \fR[\fIFOLDER\fR]"
List files whose names contain
.IR NAME ,
ignoring case, below the current directory or
.IR FOLDER .
Wildcards are accepted when quoted, as in
.BR "findfile '*.pdf'" .
.TP
.BI extract " ARCHIVE\fR..."
Unpack archives by their extension: .tar, .tar.gz, .tgz, .tar.bz2,
\&.tar.xz, .tar.zst, .zip, .gz, .bz2, .xz, .zst, .7z, .rar.
.TP
.BR recent " [\fICOUNT\fR]"
Show the most recently used commands, newest first, without repeats.
.TP
.B sysinfo
Show the operating system, kernel, uptime, CPU, memory and disk usage.
.SH SHELL COMMANDS
.BR cd ,
.BR pwd ,
.BR echo ,
.BR export ,
.BR unset ,
.BR alias ,
.BR unalias ,
.BR history ,
.BR set ,
.BR source " (also " . ),
.BR type ,
.BR exec ,
.BR true ,
.BR false ,
.BR : ,
.BR help ,
.BR exit ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR fg ,
.BR bg ,
.B wait
and
.BR kill .
Run
.B help
for a list and
.BI help " COMMAND"
for details.
.SH SYNTAX
.TP
.IB a " | " b
Pipe the output of
.I a
into
.IR b .
.TP
.IB a " && " b ", " a " || " b ", " a " ; " b
Run
.I b
if
.I a
succeeds, if it fails, or regardless.
.TP
.IB a " &"
Run
.I a
in the background.
.TP
.BR < ", " > ", " >> ", " 2> ", " &> ", " 2>&1 ", " <<<
Redirect input and output.
.TP
.BR 'text' ", " \(dqtext\(dq ", " \e
Single quotes keep text literal; double quotes allow
.B $
expansions; a backslash quotes one character.
.TP
.BR $NAME ", " ${NAME} ", " ${NAME:\-default} ", " ${#NAME}
Variables. Also
.BR ${NAME:=default} ,
.BR ${NAME:+alternative} ,
.BR ${NAME:?message} ,
and the special parameters
.BR $? ,
.BR $$ ,
.BR $! ,
.BR $# ,
.BR $@ ,
.BR $* ,
.BR $0 " to " $9 .
.TP
.BR $(command) ", " $((expression))
Command substitution and integer arithmetic.
.TP
.BR ~ ", " * ", " ? ", " [abc] ", " {a,b} ", " {1..5}
Home directory, wildcards and brace lists.
.TP
.BR !! ", " !$
The previous command, and its last word (interactive use only).
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Tab
Complete a command, file or variable.
.TP
.B Right, End
Accept the grey suggestion.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Previous and next command, matching what is already typed.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Search the history.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A, Ctrl+E
Start and end of the line.
.TP
.B Alt+B, Alt+F
Back and forward one word.
.TP
.B Ctrl+W, Ctrl+U, Ctrl+K
Delete a word, to the start, to the end.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Y
Paste what was last deleted.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Clear the screen.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Abandon the line, or stop the running command.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Suspend the running command.
.TP
.B Ctrl+D
Exit, on an empty line.
.SH CONFIGURATION
An interactive Eclipse reads
.I ~/.config/eclipse/config
(or
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/eclipse/config
if that variable is set). Each line is an ordinary command, typically
.BR alias ,
.B export
and
.BR set .
The file is ignored if it belongs to another user or is writable by
everyone.
.PP
.B set
lists every setting;
.BI set " KEY VALUE"
changes one. Settings include
.BR prompt ,
.BR prompt2 ,
.BR symbol ,
.BR git_prefix ,
.BR git_suffix ,
.BR color ,
.BR color.user ,
.BR color.host ,
.BR color.path ,
.BR color.git ,
.BR color.symbol ,
.BR color.error ,
.BR color.bracket ,
.BR color.suggestion ,
.BR autosuggest ,
.B suggest_commands
and
.BR history_size .
Run
.B help set
for the prompt format.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ECLIPSE_HISTORY
Path of the history file, overriding the default.
.TP
.B ECLIPSE_VERSION
Set by Eclipse to its version.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set and not empty, Eclipse prints no colour.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " PATH ", " PWD ", " OLDPWD ", " SHLVL ", " TERM
Used with their usual meanings.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/eclipse/config
Configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/eclipse/history
Command history (mode 600). Commands typed with a leading space are
not recorded.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of the last command run; 2 for a syntax error; 126 if a
command cannot be executed; 127 if it is not found; 128 +
.I N
if it was ended by signal
.IR N .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR sh (1)
